# The frozen legacy registries are historical artifacts pinned by sha256 in
# tests/test_legacy_registry.py, and three of the four carry CRLF line endings because they
# originated in a Windows Excel export that no longer exists.
#
# Those bytes are the thing being preserved. The risk comes from whoever clones, not from this
# repository: `.git/config` sets no core.autocrlf, but the author's global git config sets
# `autocrlf=input`, and any contributor with that setting — or with autocrlf=true on Windows — would
# have git store LF. The committed file would then differ from both the private original and the
# pinned hash, and the test would pass in a working tree that predates the commit while failing in
# every fresh clone. `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion regardless of the cloner's config,
# so what is committed, what is checked out, and what the hashes describe are the same bytes
# everywhere.
#
# Do not "clean up" these line endings. Re-normalizing them silently rewrites a frozen provenance
# record.
registry/legacy/*.csv -text
